const rejection='d11-heat-rejection',weather='d11-weather-and-operating-envelope',water='d11-water-and-heat-reuse';
const c=(key,label,options)=>({key,label,options});
const s=(id,label,title,reference,objective,explanation,extra={})=>({id,label,title,reference,objective,pedagogical_role:'mechanism',explanation:[explanation],...extra});
export const learningContract=Object.freeze({driving_question:'Where does the heat finally go, and what does moving it consume?',fixed_boundary:'Separate chiller, hot-hour site, daily tower and heat-reuse examples use their own declared boundaries.',changed_variable:'Outdoor conditions, accounting boundary, water availability or receiver demand.',primary_payoff:'Close heat, electricity and water balances before choosing an operating point.',misconception:'A closed liquid loop eliminates water use, or enough thermal capacity guarantees enough site electrical capacity.',closing_question:'Which heat path and IT operating point remain feasible during a water restriction?'});
export const initialState=Object.freeze({humidity:'dry',plant:'adiabatic',copBoundary:'compressor',condition:'hot',closedSink:'air',cycles:5,returnKnown:true,receiverHours:6,choice:'',revealed:false});
export const scenes=Object.freeze([
 s('heat-rejection-purpose','Heat leaves the site','The heat still has to leave the site.',rejection,'D11.1','Heat capture at the chip, transport through liquid or air, and final outdoor rejection are different functions. A liquid-to-liquid CDU transfers heat between circuits; it does not eliminate the outdoor duty. The same captured load can reach different rejection systems.',{pedagogical_role:'problem'}),
 s('rejection','Dry versus wet','A dry cooler heats air; a wet tower evaporates water.',rejection,'D11.1','Compare a closed dry coil with an open tower. The process liquid stays inside the dry coil. Most tower water recirculates while evaporation, blowdown and other losses require makeup. Closed equipment loops can still connect thermally to an evaporative circuit.'),
 s('plant-options','Plant operating modes','Follow the heat through each plant mode.',rejection,'D11.1','Adiabatic pads precool air before a dry coil. Air- and water-cooled classify the chiller condenser, not the fluid at the rack. Both chiller drawings use a separate 100 kW duty and 20 kW compressor input, giving 120 kW rejection. Economizers reduce or bypass compressor operation under favorable conditions; pumps and fans remain.',{controls:[c('plant','Inspect',[['adiabatic','Adiabatic pads'],['air-chiller','Air-cooled chiller'],['water-chiller','Water-cooled chiller'],['economizer','Economizer']])]}),
 s('chiller-balance','Close the chiller balance','Compressor work joins the heat going outdoors.',rejection,'D11.2','Original reader example: the evaporator collects 10 MW, the compressor consumes 2 MW and the condenser rejects 12 MW at steady state. Pumps and fans are outside this chiller boundary. Refrigeration moves heat from a colder evaporator to a warmer condenser.',{pedagogical_role:'balance'}),
 s('cooling-cop','Choose the COP boundary','The same cooling duty has two different COPs.',rejection,'D11.2','Cooling COP is cooling duty divided by the corresponding electrical input. Ten MW / 2 MW gives compressor-boundary COP 5. Including 0.5 MW of plant auxiliaries gives plant COP 4. These are heat transport ratios, not electrical conversion efficiencies. Auxiliary heat must be placed at its actual destination; the 12 MW condenser duty does not automatically include every auxiliary.',{controls:[c('copBoundary','Electrical boundary',[['compressor','Compressor only'],['plant','Whole plant']])]}),
 s('weather','Dry bulb and wet bulb','Humidity changes the evaporative opportunity.',weather,'D11.3','Hold dry bulb at 35°C and compare wet bulb at 22°C and 28°C. Wetter air reduces evaporative cooling potential. Wet bulb is an air condition; it is neither water-pipe temperature nor a universal tower outlet temperature.',{controls:[c('humidity','Outdoor air',[['dry','35°C dry / 22°C wet'],['humid','35°C dry / 28°C wet']])]}),
 s('approach-outdoors','Count every interface','Every exchanger uses part of the temperature margin.',weather,'D11.3','The reader’s synthetic 84 kW example fixes all water flows at approximately 2 kg/s, cp at 4.2 kJ/(kg K), CDU approach at 5 K and technology supply limit at 35°C. Dry cooler approach is 5 K. The wet route uses 3 K tower approach plus a separate 5 K exchanger before the CDU. The chosen approaches are stipulated inputs, not equipment curves. Humid air raises wet-route technology supply from 35°C to 41°C.',{controls:[c('humidity','Outdoor air',[['dry','35°C dry / 22°C wet'],['humid','35°C dry / 28°C wet']])]}),
 s('hot-hour','The hot-hour power budget','Cooling can fit thermally and still exhaust site power.',weather,'D11.3','At a proposed 8 MW IT load, the original reader model has 10 MW site input and 0.4 MW non-cooling overhead. Cool-bin plant COP 8 consumes 1 MW; hot-bin COP 4 consumes 2 MW. Hot thermal capacity is 8.5 MW, yet total electrical demand is 10.4 MW. Plant COP includes all cooling auxiliaries; modeled IT heat equals IT electrical input.',{controls:[c('condition','Operating condition',[['cool','Cool bin'],['hot','Hot bin']])],pedagogical_role:'counterexample'}),
 s('two-ceilings','Solve both limits','Use the lower of the electrical and thermal ceilings.',weather,'D11.3','Rearrange PIT + PIT/COP + 0.4 ≤ 10 MW. Electrical IT ceiling is 8.5333 MW in the cool bin and 7.68 MW in the hot bin. Compare with respective thermal ceilings 9 and 8.5 MW. Constant COP is stipulated within each bin; no real product curve or control transient is represented.',{controls:[c('condition','Operating condition',[['cool','Cool bin'],['hot','Hot bin']])],pedagogical_role:'balance'}),
 s('weather-bins','Keep the hours','A daily energy total must retain the hot hours.',weather,'D11.3','Twelve cool hours operate at the proposed 8 MW IT; twelve hot hours operate at 7.68 MW. The reader totals are 188.16 MWh IT, 35.04 MWh cooling, 9.6 MWh other, and 232.8 MWh site input. A daily or annual average cannot replace the hot-hour capacity constraint; no workload throughput is inferred.',{pedagogical_role:'balance'}),
 s('abilene-cooling','Abilene cooling','Abilene’s closed coolant loop rejects heat to air.',water,'D11.5','Crusoe’s August 5, 2025 design account identifies a recirculating facility-water loop and air-cooled chillers. Non-evaporative rejection avoids intentional cooling-water evaporation at that process. Fill and maintenance water remain separate. This historical design statement is not measured water use. The real Oracle aerial identifies the original Abilene campus; it does not identify cooling hardware.',{pedagogical_role:'case-study',sources:['https://www.crusoe.ai/resources/blog/an-inside-look-at-the-abilene-ai-data-center','https://www.oracle.com/data-centers/'],reviewed_on:'2026-09-16'}),
 s('closed-loop-water','Which loop uses water?','A closed rack loop can still feed an evaporative plant.',water,'D11.4','Compare a closed equipment circuit coupled to an air-cooled chiller with the same equipment circuit coupled to a water-cooled chiller and tower. The tower adds a separate makeup-water requirement. This is an architecture comparison, not a claim that Abilene changes its installed plant.',{controls:[c('closedSink','Outdoor arrangement',[['air','Air-cooled condenser'],['tower','Water-cooled + tower']])]}),
 s('water-ledger','Close the tower ledger','Makeup replaces evaporation and blowdown.',water,'D11.4','Synthetic day: evaporation E=100 m³; concentration ratio C=5 or 3. Ignore drift, leaks and storage change. Water balance M=E+B and solids balance M=CB give B=E/(C−1). At C=5, B=25 and M=125 m³/day. Lowering C to 3 raises makeup to 150 without changing assumed evaporation. These are bookkeeping inputs, not treatment settings.',{controls:[c('cycles','Concentration ratio',[[5,'C = 5'],[3,'C = 3']])],sources:['https://www.energy.gov/cmei/femp/best-management-practice-10-cooling-tower-management'],reviewed_on:'2026-09-16'}),
 s('water-metrics','Name the metric numerator','Intake and consumption answer different questions.',water,'D11.4','Keep the separate original one-day example at 100 MWh IT and 120 MWh facility input, with E=100 m³ and C=5. If 25 m³ blowdown returns to the same accounting basin after treatment, intake intensity is 1.25 L/IT kWh and consumption intensity is 1.00. Unknown return destination cannot earn a presumed return credit. Facility/IT energy is 1.20 for this day; it is not an annual PUE report. A WUE comparison must state numerator, period and accounting boundary.',{controls:[c('returnKnown','Blowdown evidence',[[true,'Same-basin return verified'],[false,'Return destination unknown']])],sources:['https://waterdata.usgs.gov/blog/nwdc-overview/'],reviewed_on:'2026-09-16'}),
 s('heat-reuse','Heat needs a customer','The customer can accept only part of the day’s heat.',water,'D11.5','Synthetic receiver: 4 MW available continuously, but a customer accepts 2 MW for six hours. At compatible temperatures with distribution losses excluded, 12 MWh of 96 MWh is accepted: 12.5%. Another destination must accept 84 MWh. If the receiver is unavailable, all 96 MWh still need rejection.',{controls:[c('receiverHours','Customer availability',[[6,'6 hours'],[0,'Closed today']])],pedagogical_role:'balance'}),
 s('reuse-interface','Qualify the receiving load','A pipe needs a temperature, a customer and a fallback.',water,'D11.5','Direct reuse needs compatible source/receiver temperatures and exchanger approach. A heat pump can raise delivery temperature by using electricity. Customer demand, delivery losses, pumps, reliability and backup rejection belong in the service agreement. Credit accepted heat, not all heat produced by the data center.'),
 s('water-restriction','A resource constraint changes the path','A water restriction can remove an otherwise capable plant.',water,'D11.5','Apply the preceding original hot-hour model in a synthetic operating brief: a tower path is unavailable because makeup supply is unavailable; a qualified air-cooled alternate retains hot-bin COP 4 and 8.5 MW thermal duty. The site is limited to 10 MW, including 0.4 MW other demand. The alternate’s rating is supplied for this exercise; it is not Abilene performance.'),
 s('heat-rejection-check','Chapter 12 decision','Which operating plan survives this hot hour?',water,'D11.5','Select a plan using the supplied hot-hour brief. An 8 MW IT load exceeds site input even though it fits thermal capacity. A 7.68 MW IT load requires 1.92 MW cooling and 0.4 MW other, totaling 10 MW. Continuing the tower ignores its unavailable makeup path. A real transition must also satisfy demonstrated flow, temperature and control limits.',{pedagogical_role:'transfer'})
]);
